# Documentation Governance

Keep documentation useful for humans and AI agents by separating intent, decisions, implementation knowledge, and evolution history.

## Document Types

- `docs/intent/`: product or feature intent, what it does, and why it matters.
- `docs/decisions/`: ADRs and decision records for non-trivial technical choices.
- `docs/knowledge/patterns/`: reusable implementation patterns.
- `docs/knowledge/anti-patterns/`: implementation shapes to avoid.
- `docs/knowledge/guides/`: step-by-step procedures.
- `docs/knowledge/reference/`: reference material, diagrams, and glossaries.
- `docs/evolution/`: relevant changes, outcomes, and learnings.

## Rules

- Create an ADR before implementing non-trivial architecture, integration, persistence, security, or API contract decisions.
- Keep one canonical source per topic and link to it instead of duplicating explanations.
- Add durable metadata such as `Created`, `Status`, and `Note` when a document is expected to live beyond one task.
- Update related docs when standards, behavior, or supported workflows change.
- Write documentation in English by default unless the user asks for another language.

## Avoid

- Putting implementation details in intent docs.
- Turning the root `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, or rules into encyclopedias.
- Creating permanent docs for trivial changes with no reusable decision or knowledge.
